WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:06.109
This is Book 09's SD-WAN design on Cisco
Catalyst SD-WAN, an implementation guide

3
00:00:06.109 --> 00:00:11.950
from the research section of the Federal
Modernization Workbook, still under review.

4
00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:16.274
Book 09 sets a vendor-neutral design
for governed branch transport,

5
00:00:16.274 --> 00:00:21.525
and its field-office chapter already uses
Catalyst edges as its costable example.

6
00:00:21.525 --> 00:00:24.120
So this guide builds on
the program's platform,

7
00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:26.988
consolidating rather than
adding another vendor.

8
00:00:26.988 --> 00:00:30.501
It's an example, not an
endorsement by any agency.

9
00:00:30.501 --> 00:00:35.988
In about fifteen minutes we'll cover the
emergency directive that gates the build,

10
00:00:35.988 --> 00:00:40.507
the hosted-or-on-premises decision,
the twelve-hub fabric, hardening,

11
00:00:40.507 --> 00:00:45.188
the TIC and Microsoft 365 paths, and
the evidence the fabric has to emit.

12
00:00:45.188 --> 00:00:47.800
First, why start with a directive?

13
00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:53.688
Most build plans start with hardware.

14
00:00:53.688 --> 00:00:57.244
This one starts with a directive,
because the product we're

15
00:00:57.244 --> 00:01:00.800
building on was the subject of
a 2026 emergency directive.

16
00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:05.829
Book 09 already says the governor has
its own exposure: one control plane

17
00:01:05.829 --> 00:01:11.025
with authority over every edge, and an
internet-facing edge in every office.

18
00:01:11.025 --> 00:01:14.063
On Catalyst SD-WAN that
isn't hypothetical.

19
00:01:14.063 --> 00:01:19.310
CISA's catalog now lists nine exploited
flaws in the platform, and every 2026

20
00:01:19.310 --> 00:01:25.050
advisory names Cisco's hosted FedRAMP
environment among the affected deployments.

21
00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:28.416
So the first gate is proving
the control plane is patched,

22
00:01:28.416 --> 00:01:32.212
hunted and hardened, before it
carries a single production flow.

23
00:01:32.212 --> 00:01:34.420
Here's what the directive required.

24
00:01:38.170 --> 00:01:42.570
Emergency Directive 26-03
landed on February 25, 2026,

25
00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:49.370
and it was strict about order: identify
every Manager and Controller, collect logs

26
00:01:49.370 --> 00:01:55.670
and forensic images, update to Cisco's
fixed releases, hunt, harden, and report.

27
00:01:55.670 --> 00:01:57.257
The clock ran in hours.

28
00:01:57.257 --> 00:02:00.550
The inventory was due
to CISA the next night,

29
00:02:00.550 --> 00:02:04.020
and the fixed releases by
five PM on February 27.

30
00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:06.782
A March update added log delivery to CLAW,

31
00:02:06.782 --> 00:02:09.545
CISA's log warehouse, and a fourth report.

32
00:02:09.545 --> 00:02:11.090
Those deadlines are past,

33
00:02:11.090 --> 00:02:14.695
and whether a new fabric must
report is a question for CISA.

34
00:02:14.695 --> 00:02:19.312
The program's position is simpler: a
new build runs the same sequence anyway,

35
00:02:19.312 --> 00:02:23.482
because it's the best published
definition of a clean control plane.

36
00:02:23.482 --> 00:02:25.940
And the list of flaws kept growing.

37
00:02:29.690 --> 00:02:32.453
The directive started
with two catalog entries.

38
00:02:32.453 --> 00:02:38.364
Today CISA's Known Exploited Vulnerabilities
catalog lists nine for Catalyst SD-WAN.

39
00:02:38.364 --> 00:02:41.523
Six carry the directive's
mitigation instructions,

40
00:02:41.523 --> 00:02:44.240
and three more arrived
in June and September.

41
00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:47.655
Two are authentication bypasses
that reach the Manager,

42
00:02:47.655 --> 00:02:49.690
the Controller and the Validator.

43
00:02:49.690 --> 00:02:54.090
The newest, added on September 30,
came with a three-day due date.

44
00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:56.103
So this isn't a one-time cleanup.

45
00:02:56.103 --> 00:02:59.750
Nine exploited flaws in eight
months is a patch tempo,

46
00:02:59.750 --> 00:03:03.315
not an event; treat the
catalog as a standing posture.

47
00:03:03.315 --> 00:03:08.039
Cisco now publishes advisories on the
first and third Wednesday of each month,

48
00:03:08.039 --> 00:03:12.327
with a week's notice, and still goes
out of cycle for active exploitation.

49
00:03:12.327 --> 00:03:14.270
So which release do you need?

50
00:03:18.020 --> 00:03:22.361
Reading Cisco's advisories together
gives a floor: a minimum release per

51
00:03:22.361 --> 00:03:26.920
software train, one for the Manager and
one for the Controller and Validator.

52
00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:31.758
That's the program's inference from Cisco's
tables, so re-read them at build time.

53
00:03:31.758 --> 00:03:33.120
A few things stand out.

54
00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:38.245
Several older trains have no fix of their
own, and Cisco's answer is to migrate.

55
00:03:38.245 --> 00:03:41.296
The newest train isn't
listed for the Controller

56
00:03:41.296 --> 00:03:44.270
and Validator in three
advisories, so ask Cisco.

57
00:03:44.270 --> 00:03:50.143
And the edges have their own catalog
entries, for the web interface and SNMP,

58
00:03:50.143 --> 00:03:55.258
so the HTTP server stays off and
SNMPv3 sits behind an access list.

59
00:03:55.258 --> 00:03:57.958
Cisco's Live Protect
shields can bridge a gap,

60
00:03:57.958 --> 00:04:01.096
but Cisco calls them
temporary partial protection.

61
00:04:01.096 --> 00:04:02.770
Next, the hunt.

62
00:04:06.520 --> 00:04:09.912
Patching doesn't tell you whether
someone already came through,

63
00:04:09.912 --> 00:04:12.408
and CISA's supplemental
guidance is sobering.

64
00:04:12.408 --> 00:04:15.578
The original bypass was
exploited as a zero-day,

65
00:04:15.578 --> 00:04:20.293
the attacker's rogue device looked
like a legitimate SD-WAN component,

66
00:04:20.293 --> 00:04:25.008
and the activity likely began in 2023,
so the hunt reaches back that far.

67
00:04:25.008 --> 00:04:30.375
The six-part hunt looks for downgrades
and reboots, rogue peering, SSH key abuse,

68
00:04:30.375 --> 00:04:32.439
root sign-ins and cleared logs,

69
00:04:32.439 --> 00:04:36.733
and any root compromise means
rebuilding from patched images.

70
00:04:36.733 --> 00:04:41.222
The hardening is concrete: five-minute
timeouts, pairwise IPsec keys,

71
00:04:41.222 --> 00:04:46.420
an enterprise certificate on the Manager,
and SIEM alerts on unexpected peering.

72
00:04:46.420 --> 00:04:49.234
The fabric carries no
production traffic until

73
00:04:49.234 --> 00:04:51.907
every line of the gate
is closed and evidenced.

74
00:04:51.907 --> 00:04:54.070
Now, the first decision.

75
00:04:57.820 --> 00:05:00.657
First, decide where the
control plane lives.

76
00:05:00.657 --> 00:05:06.101
Cisco's FedRAMP environment, Catalyst
SD-WAN for Government, is certified at

77
00:05:06.101 --> 00:05:11.458
Moderate, runs in AWS GovCloud, and is
run by a Cisco federal operations team.

78
00:05:11.458 --> 00:05:13.471
The guide recommends it by default.

79
00:05:13.471 --> 00:05:18.259
CISA's own hardening guidance points
there, Cisco patches the control plane,

80
00:05:18.259 --> 00:05:20.803
the boundary question gets an inherited,

81
00:05:20.803 --> 00:05:23.945
certified answer, and a
fourteen-VM build goes away.

82
00:05:23.945 --> 00:05:29.193
Choose on-premises if the system is High
or isolated, if the authorizing official

83
00:05:29.193 --> 00:05:31.387
needs custody of forensic images,

84
00:05:31.387 --> 00:05:34.520
or if Cisco can't commit to
the catalog's due dates.

85
00:05:34.520 --> 00:05:36.670
But hosting doesn't remove the gate.

86
00:05:36.670 --> 00:05:40.523
The hosted environment was
affected too, and the edges,

87
00:05:40.523 --> 00:05:44.633
logs, allow-lists, roles and
single sign-on stay the agency's.

88
00:05:44.633 --> 00:05:46.600
Next, the fabric.

89
00:05:50.350 --> 00:05:54.475
Here's Book 09's target: two
data centers, ten regional hubs,

90
00:05:54.475 --> 00:05:58.425
and about fifteen hundred
field offices on an overlay.

91
00:05:58.425 --> 00:06:03.277
On Catalyst, that's one edge per office,
a high-availability pair at each hub,

92
00:06:03.277 --> 00:06:07.413
and hub-and-spoke to a regional
hub, with a second hub as fallback.

93
00:06:07.413 --> 00:06:12.073
In plain terms, the Manager is where
you configure and monitor, the Validator

94
00:06:12.073 --> 00:06:13.700
decides which edges get in,

95
00:06:13.700 --> 00:06:17.251
and the Controller hands every
edge its routes and policy.

96
00:06:17.251 --> 00:06:22.313
On-premises, Cisco's sizing for this
fleet is seven VMs per data center,

97
00:06:22.313 --> 00:06:26.488
fourteen in all, which matches
Book 09's bill of materials.

98
00:06:26.488 --> 00:06:29.672
The Manager cluster tops
out at two thousand devices,

99
00:06:29.672 --> 00:06:32.350
so this fleet uses about
three quarters of it.

100
00:06:32.350 --> 00:06:33.950
Plan the growth trigger now.

101
00:06:33.950 --> 00:06:35.580
Next, policy.

102
00:06:39.330 --> 00:06:42.868
Book 09 splits branch
traffic into two classes.

103
00:06:42.868 --> 00:06:49.305
Lean is the latency-sensitive kind: sign-in,
voice, clock sync and interactive work.

104
00:06:49.305 --> 00:06:52.505
Bulk is backups, patch waves and re-syncs.

105
00:06:52.505 --> 00:06:56.155
On Catalyst, both become
application-aware routing.

106
00:06:56.155 --> 00:07:00.418
Lean gets a custom SLA class built
from the pilot's measurements,

107
00:07:00.418 --> 00:07:05.405
not Cisco's defaults; it prefers the
internet circuit and can fall back to 5G.

108
00:07:05.405 --> 00:07:08.025
Bulk stays on the internet circuit,

109
00:07:08.025 --> 00:07:12.180
kept off 5G by data policy and
behind lean traffic by QoS.

110
00:07:12.180 --> 00:07:15.427
Every path that can reach
the internet lands on the

111
00:07:15.427 --> 00:07:18.443
same security policy, so
no path gets less rigor.

112
00:07:18.443 --> 00:07:23.984
And lane discipline carries over unchanged:
the program observes and emits evidence,

113
00:07:23.984 --> 00:07:27.418
while the Manager and
Controllers route and enforce.

114
00:07:27.418 --> 00:07:29.550
Next, segmentation.

115
00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:37.025
Segmentation follows Book
09's admission design.

116
00:07:37.025 --> 00:07:41.925
Transport and management each get their
own VPN, with management out of band.

117
00:07:41.925 --> 00:07:45.425
Then each admission segment
becomes a service VPN.

118
00:07:45.425 --> 00:07:48.688
Production reaches the hubs
and the security service.

119
00:07:48.688 --> 00:07:52.601
Remediation reaches only update
and antivirus endpoints.

120
00:07:52.601 --> 00:07:54.213
Quarantine stays local.

121
00:07:54.213 --> 00:07:57.850
IoT and OT reach only their
own management systems.

122
00:07:57.850 --> 00:08:01.775
And guests go out through the security
service, with no route inward.

123
00:08:01.775 --> 00:08:06.070
Cisco ISE plugs in two ways:
security group tags on the edge,

124
00:08:06.070 --> 00:08:08.575
or identity-based firewall rules.

125
00:08:08.575 --> 00:08:14.575
One catch to remember: Cisco says TrustSec
isn't supported with FIPS mode on.

126
00:08:14.575 --> 00:08:16.682
So if the edges must run FIPS,

127
00:08:16.682 --> 00:08:21.538
segment by VPN and leave tag
enforcement to the access switches.

128
00:08:21.538 --> 00:08:23.370
Now, certificates.

129
00:08:27.120 --> 00:08:30.001
Out of the box, Catalyst
trusts Cisco's own PKI,

130
00:08:30.001 --> 00:08:34.907
and hardware edges prove who they are
with a certificate in their trust chip.

131
00:08:34.907 --> 00:08:38.958
An edge joins only if its serial
number is on the authorized list.

132
00:08:38.958 --> 00:08:44.559
Book 09 wants edges onboarded with certificates
from the agency's PKI, and Cisco's

133
00:08:44.559 --> 00:08:49.994
Enterprise Certificate option makes that
possible on recent IOS XE releases.

134
00:08:49.994 --> 00:08:51.545
The catch is renewal.

135
00:08:51.545 --> 00:08:53.320
It's automatic at onboarding,

136
00:08:53.320 --> 00:08:56.795
but Cisco says later renewals
need manual intervention.

137
00:08:56.795 --> 00:09:01.815
So this is a build plus a procedure, not
a setting: issue through the CLM platform,

138
00:09:01.815 --> 00:09:04.827
inventory certificates
from the Manager's API,

139
00:09:04.827 --> 00:09:09.307
and write renewal as a runbook
triggered by the Manager's expiry alarm.

140
00:09:09.307 --> 00:09:12.407
The CyberArk certificate
guide covers that platform.

141
00:09:12.407 --> 00:09:14.430
Next, FIPS.

142
00:09:18.180 --> 00:09:22.524
If an acquisition has to state
a FIPS 140-3 expectation,

143
00:09:22.524 --> 00:09:25.130
the honest answer has two halves.

144
00:09:25.130 --> 00:09:28.051
The control components are in good shape:

145
00:09:28.051 --> 00:09:33.376
FIPS mode is on by default on recent
releases and in the hosted environment,

146
00:09:33.376 --> 00:09:38.530
and Cisco's letter for the current
train cites an active FIPS 140-3 module.

147
00:09:38.530 --> 00:09:39.893
The edges are different.

148
00:09:39.893 --> 00:09:42.199
Their crypto modules are validated,

149
00:09:42.199 --> 00:09:46.736
but Cisco's current letters don't name
SD-WAN mode, NIAP's router listings

150
00:09:46.736 --> 00:09:51.580
don't mention it, and Cisco's own example
shows an edge with FIPS mode disabled.

151
00:09:51.580 --> 00:09:56.855
So get edge validation in SD-WAN mode as
a written vendor commitment before award.

152
00:09:56.855 --> 00:09:58.617
There's no SD-WAN STIG either;

153
00:09:58.617 --> 00:10:02.293
the router STIGs are a reference
baseline, not a checklist.

154
00:10:02.293 --> 00:10:04.510
Next, TIC 3.0.

155
00:10:08.260 --> 00:10:13.560
Book 09 names four branch egress
patterns, and each has a Catalyst build.

156
00:10:13.560 --> 00:10:17.603
Pattern A, the hairpin, sends
internet traffic to the regional hub,

157
00:10:17.603 --> 00:10:20.672
which breaks out through
its own security tunnel.

158
00:10:20.672 --> 00:10:24.248
That's every office's starting
state, and its fallback.

159
00:10:24.248 --> 00:10:27.579
Pattern B, bare local
breakout, is never allowed,

160
00:10:27.579 --> 00:10:30.910
so audit the policy group
for it on every change.

161
00:10:30.910 --> 00:10:36.738
Pattern C, the reference, is automatic
IPsec tunnels from each edge to a security

162
00:10:36.738 --> 00:10:39.829
service edge, with Microsoft 365 Optimize

163
00:10:39.829 --> 00:10:43.273
traffic going direct only
under its exception.

164
00:10:43.273 --> 00:10:46.415
Pattern D puts policy
in the endpoint agent,

165
00:10:46.415 --> 00:10:49.646
and the edge keeps
agentless devices on A or C.

166
00:10:49.646 --> 00:10:55.322
Cisco's own TIC guide predates the current
use case, so build to Book 09's reading.

167
00:10:55.322 --> 00:10:57.850
Next, the security service.

168
00:11:01.600 --> 00:11:06.543
For the security service, Cisco's government
option is Secure Access for Government,

169
00:11:06.543 --> 00:11:11.063
certified at High, with automatic tunnels
documented from a Catalyst branch.

170
00:11:11.063 --> 00:11:15.937
But it runs in one region, Virginia, and
a western office pays that round trip.

171
00:11:15.937 --> 00:11:20.237
Measure it in the pilot, or choose a
service with nearer points of presence.

172
00:11:20.237 --> 00:11:24.018
Microsoft's Global Secure
Access is available in GCC,

173
00:11:24.018 --> 00:11:29.603
and Catalyst is a listed partner, but
Cisco's guide forwards only the Microsoft

174
00:11:29.603 --> 00:11:35.101
365 profile, and Conditional Access
applies only to users running the client.

175
00:11:35.101 --> 00:11:40.400
So it carries branch Microsoft
365 traffic, not a full Pattern C.

176
00:11:40.400 --> 00:11:44.540
And in GCC, informed network
routing is still closed,

177
00:11:44.540 --> 00:11:49.162
so Microsoft 365 path choice
rests on the edge's own probes.

178
00:11:49.162 --> 00:11:50.910
Next, evidence.

179
00:11:54.660 --> 00:11:59.498
Book 09 expects decision records that
become boundary-change evidence.

180
00:11:59.498 --> 00:12:04.660
On Catalyst, they're derived from the
Manager's APIs, and labeled as derived.

181
00:12:04.660 --> 00:12:10.355
Device inventory, the audit log, alarms and
path statistics give you the controller,

182
00:12:10.355 --> 00:12:15.060
the selected path, the SLA state,
failovers and the policy references.

183
00:12:15.060 --> 00:12:18.156
Logs leave the box over
TLS syslog to the SIEM,

184
00:12:18.156 --> 00:12:21.510
and all SD-WAN syslog goes
to CISA through CLAW.

185
00:12:21.510 --> 00:12:23.773
One caveat for Sentinel users.

186
00:12:23.773 --> 00:12:28.861
The documented connector's syslog path,
through the Azure Monitor Agent, is fine.

187
00:12:28.861 --> 00:12:33.160
Its NetFlow path uses an API
whose support ended in September.

188
00:12:33.160 --> 00:12:36.923
Ingestion continues, but
treat that path as legacy.

189
00:12:36.923 --> 00:12:39.670
Next, how the build is sequenced.

190
00:12:43.420 --> 00:12:48.582
The build follows Book 09's own
sequence, and each step leaves evidence.

191
00:12:48.582 --> 00:12:50.908
Step zero is the gate we've covered.

192
00:12:50.908 --> 00:12:55.003
Phase zero, the first six months,
decides hosted or on-premises,

193
00:12:55.003 --> 00:12:58.136
sets the release baseline,
runs a ten-site pilot,

194
00:12:58.136 --> 00:13:02.232
and starts the CLAW coordination
and the static-address plan.

195
00:13:02.232 --> 00:13:04.209
Phase one builds the twelve hubs,

196
00:13:04.209 --> 00:13:08.748
with SLA classes set from the pilot's
measurements, and no office gets local

197
00:13:08.748 --> 00:13:13.433
internet until its security tunnel is
up and its boundary change is approved.

198
00:13:13.433 --> 00:13:16.997
Phase two moves offices
in waves at MPLS renewal,

199
00:13:16.997 --> 00:13:21.007
retiring MPLS only after that
office's logs reach CLAW.

200
00:13:21.007 --> 00:13:27.221
Phase three adds the Microsoft 365 direct
path and tracks the five retirement gates.

201
00:13:27.221 --> 00:13:29.010
So what does this change?

202
00:13:32.760 --> 00:13:36.398
Three findings update Book
09's planning inputs.

203
00:13:36.398 --> 00:13:40.348
First, CISA expects a static
address on every edge.

204
00:13:40.348 --> 00:13:44.746
That conflicts with zero-touch
provisioning from dynamic addresses,

205
00:13:44.746 --> 00:13:47.248
and probably with the 5G secondary path.

206
00:13:47.248 --> 00:13:50.984
It reinforces Book 09's
deterministic-egress action,

207
00:13:50.984 --> 00:13:53.447
and it belongs in the circuit order.

208
00:13:53.447 --> 00:13:57.890
Second, Book 09's cost model assumes
an on-premises control plane,

209
00:13:57.890 --> 00:14:00.823
while CISA's guidance points to GovCloud.

210
00:14:00.823 --> 00:14:03.542
Hosting replaces the fourteen-VM line with

211
00:14:03.542 --> 00:14:06.185
a subscription the
model doesn't price yet.

212
00:14:06.185 --> 00:14:08.337
Third, the catalog keeps growing,

213
00:14:08.337 --> 00:14:13.198
with the Validator now in scope and a
floor that moves with every addition.

214
00:14:13.198 --> 00:14:17.250
And one planning number: about four
hundred seventy-six more edges,

215
00:14:17.250 --> 00:14:19.460
and the fleet needs a second overlay.

216
00:14:19.460 --> 00:14:21.270
Next, the risks.

217
00:14:25.020 --> 00:14:26.683
Two risks are rated high.

218
00:14:26.683 --> 00:14:30.322
The control plane collects new
exploited flaws faster than

219
00:14:30.322 --> 00:14:34.107
most agencies can patch, and a
compromise may predate the build.

220
00:14:34.107 --> 00:14:37.992
The answers are the hosted fabric
with a written patch commitment,

221
00:14:37.992 --> 00:14:41.311
or a standing patch crew on
Cisco's publication schedule,

222
00:14:41.311 --> 00:14:44.207
and hunting before you
trust any existing fabric.

223
00:14:44.207 --> 00:14:49.159
The medium risks are ones we've met:
forensic custody on a hosted fabric,

224
00:14:49.159 --> 00:14:54.707
static addressing against zero-touch and
5G, a single-region security service,

225
00:14:54.707 --> 00:14:59.146
manual certificate renewal, and
unconfirmed edge FIPS status.

226
00:14:59.146 --> 00:15:02.357
The vendor asks turn those
into questions with owners.

227
00:15:02.357 --> 00:15:07.483
Cisco gets most of them; Microsoft,
the carriers and CISA get one each.

228
00:15:07.483 --> 00:15:09.720
Next, the example code.

229
00:15:13.470 --> 00:15:17.383
The guide comes with a small
example, a read-only Python script.

230
00:15:17.383 --> 00:15:22.212
It checks every control component against
the fixed releases for the directive

231
00:15:22.212 --> 00:15:26.682
and the catalog, and writes a gate file
that must show no fail or confirm rows.

232
00:15:26.682 --> 00:15:30.955
It exports a fleet inventory record
per device, and an audit record per

233
00:15:30.955 --> 00:15:35.744
audit-log entry, flagging sign-ins and
sources outside the management network.

234
00:15:35.744 --> 00:15:40.807
It doesn't build path-decision records;
those need the policy and statistics reads.

235
00:15:40.807 --> 00:15:44.648
And it's read-only on purpose,
because of lane discipline:

236
00:15:44.648 --> 00:15:47.570
the program observes, the fabric enforces.

237
00:15:47.570 --> 00:15:49.520
Be clear about its status, though.

238
00:15:49.520 --> 00:15:53.299
It's illustrative: unit-tested
against mocked responses,

239
00:15:53.299 --> 00:15:55.470
not proven against a live fabric.

240
00:15:55.470 --> 00:15:58.100
Finally, what to do with all of this.

241
00:16:01.850 --> 00:16:04.819
To move this guide from
under review to proved,

242
00:16:04.819 --> 00:16:08.725
build the gate and the ten-site
pilot in a lab or a pilot fabric,

243
00:16:08.725 --> 00:16:12.475
run the example script against
it, and record the evidence.

244
00:16:12.475 --> 00:16:16.931
Some things couldn't be verified,
like a Cisco recommended release

245
00:16:16.931 --> 00:16:21.063
and edge FIPS status in SD-WAN
mode, so confirm those with Cisco.

246
00:16:21.063 --> 00:16:25.078
The decisions are hosted or
on-premises, the release baseline,

247
00:16:25.078 --> 00:16:29.487
static addressing, which security
service, and the renewal runbook.

248
00:16:29.487 --> 00:16:34.213
For why the program builds on this platform,
read the SD-WAN platform deep dive.

249
00:16:34.213 --> 00:16:38.601
For the design itself, read Book
09's chapters on governed transport

250
00:16:38.601 --> 00:16:43.225
and the field-office transformation,
or watch the SD-WAN technical deck.

251
00:16:43.225 --> 00:16:44.400
Thanks for listening.
